// Sample family: every person and record cited here is fictional.
// Short cited paragraphs stay whole so Typst never sets a footnote before its marker.

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
John Penhallow was baptized at St Just in Penwith on 6 April 1845, the son of Richard Penhallow, a miner, and his wife, Grace, of Trewellard.#src("john-baptism-1845") A study of St Just families published in 1998 describes a parish of tin and copper mines, many of whose men left Cornwall when the mines failed.#src("john-journal-1998")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Elizabeth Ann Trembath came from Camborne. The same study says she was the daughter of James Trembath, a copper miner, and was baptized there in 1847.#src("elizabeth-birth-camborne") By the time she married, her father had died and she was living in Penzance.#src("elizabeth-age-at-marriage-1866")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
John and Elizabeth married at the Wesleyan Methodist Chapel in Penzance on 9 October 1866.#src("penhallow-trembath-marriage-1866") He was twenty-one, a tin miner from St Just, and she was nineteen. Richard Penhallow and Mary Trembath were the witnesses. John signed his name, and Elizabeth made her mark.#src("penhallow-trembath-marriage-1866")
]

// cite-lint: ok figure caption — identifies the facsimile and says it is fictional
#fig("documents/plates/1866-penhallow-trembath-marriage-plate.jpg", caption: "The marriage entry of John Penhallow and Elizabeth Ann Trembath, Penzance, 9 October 1866. A facsimile made for the Flyleaf sample; the record is fictional.")

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Their first child, Richard John, was born at Penzance on 2 September 1867.#src("richard-penhallow-1867::richard-birth-1867") The next spring the three of them sailed from Liverpool. A passenger list records their arrival in New York on 14 May 1868: John, twenty-three, a miner; Elizabeth, twenty-one; and Richard, eight months old, all bound for Wisconsin.#src("john-arrival-1868") Richard died at Mineral Point later that year.#src("richard-penhallow-1867::richard-death-1868")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
A county album published in 1904 says the family came to America in 1867.#src("john-album-1904") The passenger list was made at the time, and John's obituary also gives 1868, so this history follows them.#src("john-arrival-obituary")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
In 1880 the census found John working as a miner at Mineral Point.#src("john-census-1880") Elizabeth kept house, and their children Mary Jane, nine, and William Henry, two, were at home.#src("elizabeth-census-1880")#src("mary-jane-penhallow::mary-jane-census-1880") For more than thirty years John worked in the lead and zinc mines around the town, and later at the zinc works.#src("john-mining-years") He was a faithful member of the Methodist church and for many years a class leader.#src("john-methodist")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
John died at his home on Shake Rag Street on 3 March 1912, in his sixty-seventh year.#src("john-death-1912") His funeral was held at the Methodist church on 6 March, and he was buried in the city cemetery.#src("john-burial-1912") The notice named his widow, his daughter Mary Jane, of Madison, and his son William, of Dodgeville.#src("elizabeth-widow-1912")
]

// cite-lint: ok figure caption — identifies the facsimile and says it is fictional
#fig("newspapers/plates/1912-03-07-iowa-county-lantern-penhallow-obituary-plate.jpg", caption: "John Penhallow's obituary in The Iowa County Lantern, 7 March 1912. A facsimile made for the Flyleaf sample; the newspaper and the notice are fictional.")

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Elizabeth lived on at Mineral Point for almost eleven years. She died there on 21 January 1923, and her daughter-in-law Edith recorded the date in the family Bible.#src("elizabeth-death-1923")
]
